We wish to prove the following result:

Content:
[MATH: \dfrac{\textrm{d}}{\textrm{d}x}(\sech{x}) = -\sech{x} \tanh{x}.]

Content:
To do this, we can use the fact that

Content:
[MATH: \sech x = \dfrac{1}{\cosh x}.]

Content:
Differentiating the above equation using the chain rule, we arrive at

Content:
[MATH: (d)/(dx)(sech x)|= (d)/(dx)((1)/(\cosh x)); = (d)/(dx)(\cosh x)^{− 1}; = − (\cosh x)^{− 2} ⋅ (\cosh x)^{′}; = − (1)/(\cosh^{2} x) ⋅ \sinh x; = − (1)/(\cosh x) ⋅ (\sinh x)/(\cosh x); = − sech x\tanh x]

